@@ -1,35 +1,37 @@
 {
-  "id": "grammar.b1.cambiosocial.05.no-solo-sino-tambien",
-  "title": "Correlativos formales: no solo... sino también",
+  "id": "grammar.b1.cambiosocial.05.aunque",
+  "title": "Although: aunque and the limits of change",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*No solo... sino también* (not only... but also) is the formal tool for stacking two related claims into one sentence, common in a closing synthesis that wants to show a change ran deeper than it first appears. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know *aunque* (\"although\") from A2 Unit 28. It joins two facts that seem to pull in different directions. In this lesson it shows how society changed even when rights stayed limited: *Aunque las mujeres no podían votar, muchas trabajaban*."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "América Latina no solo había transformado el aspecto de sus ciudades, sino también la propia composición de su población.",
-          "english": "Latin America had not only transformed the appearance of its cities, but also the very composition of its population."
+          "spanish": "Aunque las mujeres no podían votar, muchas trabajaban como maestras.",
+          "english": "Although women could not vote, many worked as teachers."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Esta transformación no solo cambió el aspecto de las ciudades, sino también la estructura misma de su sociedad.",
-          "english": "This transformation not only changed the appearance of the cities, but also the very structure of its society."
+          "spanish": "Aunque la universidad era pública, tenía demasiado control de la Iglesia.",
+          "english": "Although the university was public, it had too much control by the Church."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Esta doble velocidad no solo generó una brecha económica entre el campo y la ciudad, sino también una brecha cultural cada vez más marcada.",
-          "english": "This double speed generated not only an economic gap between countryside and city, but also an increasingly marked cultural gap."
+          "spanish": "Aunque el poder seguía en manos de grupos pequeños, muchos pedían más derechos.",
+          "english": "Although power stayed in the hands of small groups, many were asking for more rights."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Las élites urbanas comenzaron a sentirse más cercanas a Europa que a sus propios compatriotas rurales.",
-          "english": "The urban elites began to feel closer to Europe than to their own rural compatriots."
+          "spanish": "Muchas mujeres trabajaban fuera de casa, aunque tenían menos derechos que los hombres.",
+          "english": "Many women worked outside the home, although they had fewer rights than men."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Sino* (not *sino que*) links two nouns or noun phrases, as in every example above; reach for *sino que* only when the second half needs its own conjugated verb, e.g. *no solo cambió la ciudad, sino que también cambió el campo*."
+      "content": "With facts, the verb after *aunque* is in the indicative (*no podían*, *era*). The clause with *aunque* can come first or second; put a comma after it when it comes first."
