-      "content": "Describing a country's product means packing detail into a single sentence -- *que* for a thing or action, *donde* for a place, *cuyo/cuya* for a possessive link (\"whose\") that agrees with the noun that follows it, not with the person or thing it refers back to. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know *cuyo*, *cuya*, *cuyos* and *cuyas* from B1 Unit 5. They mean \"whose\" and join two ideas about the same noun: *el café, cuyo precio subía y bajaba*. Here you use them to say what belonged to a product or a country."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Brasil, cuya producción de café llegó a superar la de cualquier otro país del mundo, construyó alrededor de este cultivo toda una estructura política y social.",
-          "english": "Brazil, whose coffee production came to surpass that of any other country in the world, built a whole political and social structure around this crop."
+          "spanish": "El café, cuyo precio subía y bajaba en el mercado mundial, era una gran riqueza.",
+          "english": "Coffee, whose price went up and down on the world market, was a great source of wealth."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El estado de São Paulo, donde se concentraba la mayor parte de las plantaciones, se volvió central en la política nacional.",
-          "english": "The state of São Paulo, where most of the plantations were concentrated, became central to national politics."
+          "spanish": "Los países cuya economía dependía de un solo producto necesitaban compradores extranjeros.",
+          "english": "Countries whose economy depended on a single product needed foreign buyers."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Chile incorporó los territorios donde se concentraban los mayores yacimientos de salitre.",
-          "english": "Chile incorporated the territories where the largest saltpeter deposits were concentrated."
+          "spanish": "Chile, cuyos yacimientos de salitre eran muy ricos, ganó la Guerra del Pacífico.",
+          "english": "Chile, whose saltpetre deposits were very rich, won the War of the Pacific."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El banano, un producto cuya exportación creció rápidamente en países como Costa Rica y Honduras, definiría la imagen exterior de la región.",
-          "english": "The banana, a product whose exports grew rapidly in countries like Costa Rica and Honduras, would define the region's image abroad."
+          "spanish": "La United Fruit Company, cuyas plantaciones de banano estaban en Centroamérica, se fundó en 1899.",
+          "english": "The United Fruit Company, whose banana plantations were in Central America, was founded in 1899."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Cuyo* agrees with the noun it introduces, not the owner: *cuya producción* (agreeing with *producción*, feminine) even though it refers back to *Brasil* (masculine) -- this trips up learners who expect it to work like an adjective describing the owner instead."
+      "content": "*Cuyo* agrees with the thing that belongs, not with the owner: *cuyo precio* (the price), *cuya economía* (the economy), *cuyos yacimientos* (the deposits), *cuyas plantaciones* (the plantations). Do not add an article or *su*: not *cuyo el precio*, not *que su precio*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-03-aunque-subjuntivo-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-03-aunque-subjuntivo-gr.json
index 5aa386dc..948371a7 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-03-aunque-subjuntivo-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-03-aunque-subjuntivo-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,37 @@
 {
-  "id": "grammar.b1.economiasexportacion.03.aunque-subjuntivo",
-  "title": "Aunque + subjuntivo: concesiones sobre el comercio",
+  "id": "grammar.b1.economiasexportacion.03.aunque",
+  "title": "Although: aunque and facts that contrast",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Aunque takes the subjunctive when you're conceding a hypothetical or a point you're granting for argument's sake rather than reporting a fact -- it lets a sentence admit a benefit (jobs were created, ports expanded) while still steering the reader toward the real point. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know *aunque* (\"although\") from A2 Unit 28. It joins two facts that seem to pull in different directions. When both are facts of the past, the verb after *aunque* is in the indicative: *Aunque la red de trenes crecía, los precios se decidían en Europa.*"
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Aunque resulte tentador imaginar los ferrocarriles como simples símbolos de progreso nacional, la mayoría fueron construidos por empresas extranjeras.",
-          "english": "Although it may be tempting to imagine the railways as simple symbols of national progress, most were built by foreign companies."
+          "spanish": "Aunque los ferrocarriles unían los puertos con el interior, muchas líneas no unían unas regiones con otras.",
+          "english": "Although the railways joined the ports with the interior, many lines did not join one region with another."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Aunque estas empresas generaran empleo local, buena parte de sus ganancias salía directamente del país.",
-          "english": "Although these companies generated local employment, much of their profits left the country directly."
+          "spanish": "Aunque la red de trenes crecía, los precios de los productos se decidían en los mercados de Europa.",
+          "english": "Although the railway network was growing, the prices of the products were decided in the markets of Europe."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Su influencia se extendía incluso sobre la política chilena, aunque nunca ocupara ningún cargo oficial en el gobierno.",
-          "english": "His influence extended even over Chilean politics, even though he never held any official post in the government."
+          "spanish": "Aunque el enclave daba trabajo a mucha gente, una parte de las ganancias salía del país.",
+          "english": "Although the enclave gave work to many people, part of the profits left the country."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Los puertos se expandieron enormemente, aunque su función principal siguiera siendo la salida de materias primas.",
-          "english": "The ports expanded enormously, even though their main function continued to be the outflow of raw materials."
+          "spanish": "Muchas líneas no unían las regiones, aunque los ferrocarriles unían los puertos con el interior.",
+          "english": "Many lines did not join the regions, although the railways joined the ports with the interior."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Compare aunque + indicative (*aunque es cierto que*..., stating a fact as true) with aunque + subjunctive (*aunque sea cierto que*..., granting a point without committing to it) -- the reading above uses the subjunctive throughout because each concession is being weighed against the writer's real argument, not simply reported."
+      "content": "The *aunque* clause can come first or second. When it comes first, put a comma after it. The main idea is the clause that is *not* introduced by *aunque*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-04-pluscuamperfecto-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-04-pluscuamperfecto-gr.json
index 69d86eea..4a416ee8 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-04-pluscuamperfecto-gr.json
