 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.economiasexportacion.04.pluscuamperfecto",
-  "title": "Pluscuamperfecto: lo que ya había ocurrido",
+  "title": "The pluperfect: what had already happened",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "The pluperfect (*había* + participle) marks something that had already happened before another past moment -- essential here, where debt peonage only makes sense once you know the land privatization laws that made it possible came first. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know the pluperfect (*había* + past participle) from B1 Unit 1. It says that something had already happened before another moment in the past. In this lesson that moment is the export boom or a census: *Antes del auge, muchas familias habían trabajado tierras pequeñas.*"
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Pluperfect",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "había trabajado / habían trabajado",
+          "had worked"
+        ],
+        [
+          "había nacido / habían nacido",
+          "had been born"
+        ],
+        [
+          "había llegado / habían llegado",
+          "had arrived"
+        ]
+      ]
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Este sistema de deudas no había surgido de la nada: se apoyaba en las leyes liberales de privatización de tierras.",
-          "english": "This debt system hadn't arisen out of nowhere: it rested on the liberal land-privatization laws."
-        },
-        {
-          "spanish": "Estas leyes ya habían despojado a muchas comunidades indígenas de sus tierras comunales.",
-          "english": "These laws had already stripped many indigenous communities of their communal lands."
+          "spanish": "Antes del auge exportador, muchas familias habían trabajado tierras pequeñas.",
+          "english": "Before the export boom, many families had worked small plots of land."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Millones de inmigrantes europeos habían llegado ya, hacia finales del siglo, atraídos por la demanda de mano de obra.",
-          "english": "Millions of European immigrants had already arrived, toward the end of the century, drawn by the demand for labor."
+          "spanish": "Según el censo de 1914, alrededor del treinta por ciento de la población había nacido en el extranjero.",
+          "english": "According to the 1914 census, about thirty per cent of the population had been born abroad."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Para 1900, un pequeño número de familias había acumulado ya extensiones de tierra prácticamente inimaginables décadas antes.",
-          "english": "By 1900, a small number of families had already accumulated stretches of land practically unimaginable decades before."
+          "spanish": "Muchos inmigrantes habían llegado sin tierra y trabajaban en la cosecha del trigo.",
+          "english": "Many immigrants had arrived without land and worked in the wheat harvest."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Ya* (already) pairs naturally with the pluperfect to stress that the earlier event was fully complete by the later reference point -- *habían llegado ya*, *había acumulado ya* -- and can move around the sentence without changing its meaning."
+      "content": "Use *había* / *habían* only for the earlier event; tell the main story in the preterite or the imperfect. The participle never changes: *habían llegado*, not *habían llegados*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-05-condicional-hipotetico-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-economiasexportacion-05-condicional-hipotetico-gr.json
