 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.revolucion.01.no-es-lo-mismo",
-  "title": "No es lo mismo... que: distinciones conceptuales",
+  "title": "Relative clauses: the people who rise up",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*No es lo mismo* X que Y (X is not the same thing as Y) is the natural way to draw a sharp conceptual line -- exactly what a definition of \"revolution\" needs when the everyday and the technical sense of the word get blurred together. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know relative clauses with *que* and *quien* from B1 Unit 5. They tell us which people or things we mean. To explain what a revolution is, they are very useful: *the military who take power*, *the rulers who lose support*."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "No es lo mismo un simple cambio de gobernante que una revolución.",
-          "english": "A simple change of ruler is not the same thing as a revolution."
+          "spanish": "Los militares que toman el poder cambian al gobernante.",
+          "english": "The military who take power change the ruler."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "No es lo mismo derrocar a un presidente que transformar por completo la estructura social de un país.",
-          "english": "Overthrowing a president is not the same thing as completely transforming a country's social structure."
+          "spanish": "Los gobernantes que tienen el poder pierden el apoyo del ejército.",
+          "english": "The rulers who hold power lose the army's support."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Esta distinción entre revolución política y revolución social resulta fundamental.",
-          "english": "This distinction between political revolution and social revolution turns out to be fundamental."
+          "spanish": "Las personas que se sublevan quieren un cambio profundo.",
+          "english": "The people who rise up want a deep change."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "No es lo mismo un nuevo presidente que un nuevo país.",
-          "english": "A new president is not the same thing as a new country."
+          "spanish": "Algunas revoluciones cambiaron las empresas que eran del Estado.",
+          "english": "Some revolutions changed the companies that belonged to the state."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Both halves of *no es lo mismo* X que Y can be a noun, an infinitive, or a whole clause -- as long as the two things being contrasted are grammatically parallel, as in derrocar a un presidente (infinitive) que transformar la estructura social (infinitive)."
+      "content": "For people you can use *que* (*los militares que toman el poder*) in a clause that says which ones. In English you say \"who\" for people and \"that\" for things; Spanish uses *que* for both."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucion-02-conectores-causales-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucion-02-conectores-causales-gr.json
